From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: phy_meson_usb: log the controller reset pulse per phy

Diagnostic only. No register is written and no control flow changes.
Separate from 0015 so that one build carries exactly one behavioural
change and one added print, and the two can be read apart -- the same
split as 0010 and 0011.

The question it answers is whether both phys now pulse their controller
reset bit. 0015 changes a guard from function-static to per-instance;
nothing else on this image can show that the second write happens.
CONFIG_DEVMEM is unset so there is no /dev/mem, busybox carries no
devmem applet, this driver exposes no debugfs or sysfs register view,
and the pulse register at reset_regs + 0x00 does not hold the bit for a
later read. A print from inside the write path is the only route.

The print is unconditional, including on the path that writes nothing.
That is the point of it: if the guard still suppressed the second phy
the line would say "skipped" rather than not appear, and an absent line
is indistinguishable from the module never having been reloaded. The
two outcomes are

	usb-ctrl-reset bit=5 pulsed		usbphy0
	usb-ctrl-reset bit=4 pulsed		usbphy1	  -- 0015 works

	usb-ctrl-reset bit=5 pulsed		usbphy0
	usb-ctrl-reset bit=4 skipped		usbphy1	  -- 0015 did not

and a build carrying neither line is a build whose amlogic-usb.ko was
not the one that loaded, which is a deployment result rather than a
result about the code.

reset_level is reported as context, not as a readback of the write. It
is the level register at reset_regs + 0x40, which the pulse at
reset_regs + 0x00 does not change, so it is expected to read the same on
both lines. It is included because it is free at that instant and it
distinguishes a phy whose reset_regs mapping is wrong from one whose
guard is wrong.

What it does not establish: that the store reached the bus. It
establishes that writel() executed with 1 << 4 for the phy whose
usb-reset-bit is 4. Everything past that is the MMIO write barrier's
job, and there is no instrument on this image that can see further.

Read it with dmesg. It does not reach the serial console and cannot:
OpenRC's dmesg service sets console_loglevel to 1 during sysinit, which
runs before udev loads this module, so KERN_INFO is filtered out for the
rest of the boot. Measured on the 2026-08-26 boot -- the serial capture
ends at the last kernel line at t=1.5295 s and both of these lines, at
t=1.5488 and t=1.5494, are in dmesg and not in the capture.

Not free of effect, even so. printk formats and timestamps into the ring
buffer whether or not a console takes the record, and this one sits
between meson_u2phy_hold_reset() and the usleep_range() that follows the
pulse in meson_u2phy_aml_init(), so the timing of the rest of phy init
shifts. It is recorded so a changed outcome is not mistaken for the print
being passive.

Both existing dev_dbg() readbacks around the pulse are left as they are.
CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG is unset, nothing in this directory defines DEBUG
or DYNAMIC_DEBUG_MODULE, so they compile to dev_no_printk() and their
readl() calls sit in dead code -- promoting them would add two more
prints answering a question this one already answers.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
@@ -29,6 +29,8 @@
 /* Reset usb controller. */
 int meson_u2phy_usb_reset(struct amlogic_usb_v2 *phy)
 {
+	bool pulsed = false;
+
 	if (phy->usb_reset_bit == -1U)
 		return 0;
 
@@ -41,12 +43,41 @@
 				readl(phy->reset_regs + phy->reset_level));
 	if (!phy->usb_reset_done) {
 		phy->usb_reset_done = true;
+		pulsed = true;
 		if (phy->usb_reset_bit == 2)
 			writel((readl(phy->reset_regs) |
 				(0x1 << phy->usb_reset_bit)), phy->reset_regs);
 		else
 			writel((0x1 << phy->usb_reset_bit), phy->reset_regs);
 	}
+
+	/*
+	 * Diagnostic. Printed unconditionally, including on the path that
+	 * writes nothing, so that a failure of the guard is a line saying
+	 * "skipped" rather than an absent line -- an absence would be
+	 * indistinguishable from the module not having been reloaded.
+	 *
+	 * Every dev_dbg() in this directory compiles to dev_no_printk():
+	 * CONFIG_DYNAMIC_DEBUG is unset and nothing defines DEBUG or
+	 * DYNAMIC_DEBUG_MODULE here, so the two readbacks either side of
+	 * this block do not execute and print nothing. dev_info() is the
+	 * lowest level that reaches the kernel log at all on this build.
+	 *
+	 * Read it with dmesg, not from a serial capture. OpenRC's dmesg
+	 * service sets console_loglevel to 1 during sysinit, before udev
+	 * loads this module, so no dev_info() from this driver ever reaches
+	 * the console -- and an empty capture reads exactly like a patch
+	 * that did nothing.
+	 *
+	 * reset_level is the level register at reset_regs + 0x40; the pulse
+	 * above writes reset_regs + 0x00. It is reported as context, not as
+	 * a readback of the write -- the pulse register does not hold the
+	 * bit for a subsequent read.
+	 */
+	dev_info(phy->dev, "usb-ctrl-reset bit=%u %s reset_level=0x%x\n",
+				phy->usb_reset_bit, pulsed ? "pulsed" : "skipped",
+				readl(phy->reset_regs + phy->reset_level));
+
 	dev_dbg(phy->dev, "%s after: 0x%x.\n", __func__,
 				readl(phy->reset_regs + phy->reset_level));
 	return 0;
-- 
2.43.0
